<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The First Cities</h1><p class="path-tagline">Walls, temples, archives and planned streets: where people first lived together by the thousand.</p><p class="path-intro">For most of human history people lived in camps and villages. Then, in a few favoured places, they began to build cities: places of thousands, with temples, palaces, walls, workshops and the records to run them. This path visits early cities in the Near East, Egypt, the Indus, Peru, China and Mexico, from a spring-fed settlement at Jericho to the planned avenues of Teotihuacan, and shows how differently, and in how many separate places, urban life began.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/jericho"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jericho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Jericho</span><span class="path-step-why">Settled at Tell es-Sultan from about 9000 BCE beside its springs, one of the oldest continuously inhabited places in the world.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/uruk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warka-vase.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 3500 BCE – 1802 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Uruk</span><span class="path-step-why">The largest city in the world in the fourth millennium BCE, where temple administrators began recording goods on clay tablets around 3300 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Memphis, Egypt</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded around 3100 BCE where the Nile valley meets the Delta, Egypt's capital for much of its history, with the pyramids of the Old Kingdom in its cemeteries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mari"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/statue-of-ebih-il.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 2900 BCE – 1761 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mari</span><span class="path-step-why">For over a thousand years it controlled river traffic on the middle Euphrates; its archive of some 20,000 tablets survived Hammurabi's conquest.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/caral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caral.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Place · c. 2600–2000 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Caral</span><span class="path-step-why">Platform mounds and sunken circular plazas from about 2600 BCE make it one of the oldest cities in the Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · State</span><span class="path-step-name">Indus Valley Civilisation</span><span class="path-step-why">Planned cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, linked by sea through the Gulf to Mesopotamia, whose texts probably call the land Meluhha.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Ur</span><span class="path-step-why">Capital of the Third Dynasty of Ur, whose kings raised its great ziggurat, until the Elamites sacked it around 2004 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yinxu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Place · c. 1300–1046 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Yinxu</span><span class="path-step-why">The last Shang capital, near Anyang: palace and temple foundations, royal tombs, bronze workshops and tens of thousands of inscribed oracle bones.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/teotihuacan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 100 BCE – 550 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Teotihuacan</span><span class="path-step-why">A planned city of perhaps 100,000 people in the Valley of Mexico, among the largest in the world in the 3rd to 6th centuries CE.</span></span></a></li></ol>
